'''Calatayud''' is the capital and {{W|primate city}} of [[Andalus]]. The city makes up the central portion of the [[Plain of Calatayud]], standing on the [[Júquer River]]. With a [[Communes of Andalus|communal]] population over eleven million people in 2022, Calatayud is the largest population center in the Andalusian Republic, and the [[List of cities in the Fosia by population|? largest in Fosia]]. An Alpha+ global city, Calatayud has risen to become a center of cuisine, fashion, business, and contemporary arts in the Hydocardian region. It is known for its preserved [[w:eclecticism|eclectic]] [[#Architecture|architecture]] and rich [[w:culture|cultural life]]. 

Calatayud is a {{W|cosmopolitanism|cosmopolitan}} metropolis that is home to thousands of ethnic and religious groups, contributing to its culture as well as to the [[Castejón dialect|dialect spoken in the city]] and in some other parts of the country. This is because since the 19th century, the city (and the country in general), has been a major recipient of millions of [[Immigration to Andalus|immigrants from all over the world]], making it a {{W|cultural mosaic}} where thousands of ethnic groups live together. 

Calatayud history spans 2x centuries, and is one of the oldest [[List of oldest continuously inhabited cities|continuously inhabited Hydocardian cities]], having been founded by [[Aeolia|Aeolian]] settlers in 800BC. Concurrently, it been a economic and political center in Hesperia since ancient times. Eventually, the city successively became the capital of the [[Hesperian League]], the [[Andalusian Empire]] and the current Republic, and is regarded by many as one of the first Imperial cities and ''metropoli''. It became the ''{{W|de facto}}'' capital of Andalus in the 1088 [[Treaty of <>]]). In addition, the city is the seat of the Hesperian legislature, the [[General Junta]], housed at the [[Andalusian Cabildo]] in the [[Capitolino]] district. While it has experienced rapid growth and modernization, especially with the [[Hydocardian Exposition]] of 1988 and the [[Expo '92]], it has preserved the look and feel of several historic neighborhoods and streets. Its {{W|old town}}, with an area of {{convert|14|km2|0}}, contains three [[AESCO]] {{W|World Heritage Sites}}: the {{W|Alcázar of Seville|Alcázar palace complex}}, the [[Basilica of Calatayud|Calatayud Basilica]] and the [[Archivo General de Hydocardia|General Archive of Hydocardia]].

The city is one of the most substantial economic engines within the nation, with a diverse economy in a broad range of professional and cultural fields. 25% of the city is designated or protected green space, one of the world's highest proportions. Its economy is based on high-tech firms and the service sector, encompassing a diverse range of creative industries, research facilities, media corporations and convention venues. Significant industries also include IT, pharmaceuticals, biomedical engineering, clean tech, biotechnology, construction and electronics. Today, the city is known as the heart of [[Silicon Coast]], due to the concentration of tech companies, venture capital, consumer electronics developers, and gaming corporations. The city has consistently ranked highly as one of the world’s most livable, reflecting its commitment to sustainable development, [[Calatayud Metro|advanced public transportation systems]], comprehensive social policies, and a [[Andalusian values|progressive cultural and political climate]]. 
